me\": \"MVD series\"}}\n4. {role: \"flow transmitter\", type_designations: [\"5700*1(2,3 or 5)A***ZZ*\"], characteristics: {...}}\n5. {role: \"electronic calculating and indicating device\", type_designations: [\"Multiload II\"], characteristics: {\"manufacturer\": \"Toptech Systems, Inc\"}}\n6. {role: \"electronic calculating and indicating device\", type_designations: [\"MASTERLOAD.iQ\", \"MASTERLOADx.iQ\"], characteristics: {\"manufacturer\": \"Liquid Controls LLC\"}}\n7. {role: \"gas extractor\", type_designations: [\"EGS_X\"], characteristics: {\"manufacturer\": \"S.A.M.P.I. s.p.a., Italy\"}}\n\nAlso the general construction list: \"Each measuring instrument consists at least of: One pump; One measurement transducer (meter); One calculating/indicating device (calculator); Optional one gas elimination device (gas extractor).\" This describes composition — could go in certified_type.description or as part of description. I'll add to description maybe. The description field is free-text; I can include both sentences. But keep it reasonable. I'll include the main description and maybe the \"consists at least of\" as part of description.\n\nCharacteristics type_level:\n- accuracy_class: values 0.3/0.5/1.0 — this is a list of accuracy classes. Since accuracy classes apply to the cert overall, put in recommendation.accuracy_classes. Also could be type_level characteristic with value as list? The structured value rules say lists of values → model matrix. But accuracy classes aren't per-model. I'd put it in recommendation.accuracy_classes: [0.3, 0.5, 1.0]. Maybe also as type_level characteristic \"accuracy_class\" with value... Hmm, value must be number|string|{min,max}. A list isn't allowed. Skip type_level accuracy_class; keep in recommendation.\n- viscosity: \"Liquid other than water with viscosity in the range 0,3 - 1242 mPa·s\" → characteristic: measured_liquid_viscosity? Or \"intended_for_measurement\". Better: characteristic \"viscosity_range\": value {min: 0.3, max: 1242}, unit \"mPa·s\" (unit_symbol \"mPa·s\"). And \"intended_for_measurement\": \"Liquids other than water (excluding food liquids)\".\n\nFootnote: \"\\*) The actual characteristics are depending on the configuration and applicable parts chosen for the complete measuring system.\" → footnotes[] with marker \"*\". The accuracy class and viscosity have \"*\" marker. So footnote_markers: [\"*\"] on those values.\n\nconfig_level: The characteristics depend on configuration — but no specific config axis data given here. Skip.\n\nModel family: not explicitly given (types vary). The report list suggests multiple models, but no explicit model matrix in this fragment. Skip model_family.\n\nRevision history: \"revision 0\" but no changes list. revision_history: maybe [{revision: \"0\", date: \"7 April 2022\", changes: null}]? Don't fabricate changes. The date issued is 7 April 2022. I could include revision_history with revision \"0\" and date. Hmm, \"changes\" not present. Rule 8: omit fields not present. I could include revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"0\", \"date\": \"2022-04-07\"}] without changes. That seems safe. Dates: should I normalize to ISO? The markdown has \"7 April 2022\". Schema doesn't specify format. I'll use ISO format \"2022-04-07\" for dates — it's cleaner. For test report dates too.\n\nDate format decision: The schema examples don't show date formats. I'll use ISO 8601 (YYYY-MM-DD). All dates convertible: 8 August 2018 → 2018-08-08; 5 April 2019 → 2019-04-05; 10 July 2002 → 2002-07-10; 1 March 2003 → 2003-03-01; 21 May 2005 → 2005-05-21; 19 December 2006 → 2006-12-19; 26 April 2007 → 2007-04-26; 17 December 2009 → 2009-12-17; 5 April 2011 → 2011-04-05; 20 November 2011 → 2011-11-20; 19 December 2017 → 2017-12-19; 21 February 2005 → 2005-02-21; 29 January 2008 → 2008-01-29; 19 November 2008 → 2008-11-19; 17 May 2011 → 2011-05-17; 20 October 2011 → 2011-10-20; 13 February 2012 → 2012-02-13; 23 May 2012 → 2012-05-23; 10 January 2013 → 2013-01-10; 4 